>
  <p class="note">
    Routine inspections are not building, pest or specialist compliance inspections. Their purpose and legal entry rules
    are different. Use qualified specialists where a property issue requires specialist assessment.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Victorian entry rules say</h2>
  <p>
    Consumer Affairs Victoria describes a general inspection as a routine inspection used to check that the property is
    in good condition. A general inspection can only occur after the first three months of the rental agreement and,
    ordinarily, no more frequently than every six months.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a general inspection, the renter must be given the required written notice. Consumer Affairs Victoria currently
    lists seven days as the minimum notice period for a routine inspection, with rules about permitted entry times and
    how notice is given.
  </p>

  <h2>What a useful owner report should help you understand</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Whether the visible condition of the property has materially changed since the previous inspection.</li>
    <li>Maintenance items that need action now, monitoring, or specialist assessment.</li>
    <li>Any visible damage or wear that deserves context rather than a vague photograph.</li>
    <li>Whether previously reported maintenance has actually been completed.</li>
    <li>Clear action items: what happens next, who is responsible and whether owner approval is required.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Photos are useful; context is better</h2>
  <p>
    A gallery of images can document condition, but it should not force the owner to diagnose the property from
    photographs. Useful reporting explains why a photo matters and separates ordinary wear, maintenance concerns and
    matters that may need specialist input.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions worth asking your property manager</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>How are maintenance items tracked after the inspection report is sent?</li>
    <li>How are unresolved items carried forward to the next inspection?</li>
    <li>What triggers an owner call rather than simply appearing in a report?</li>
    <li>How do you distinguish a routine observation from something that requires a qualified trade or specialist?</li>
    <li>Will I know what action has been agreed and when it is complete?</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The inspection is part of a larger management rhythm</h2>
  <p>
    A six-monthly inspection cannot replace day-to-day property management. Repairs, renter communication, lease
    milestones, arrears and changing property circumstances happen between inspections. The inspection should strengthen
    that ongoing picture—not operate as a disconnected event.
  </p>
